WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.019 --> 00:00:14.969
If you, If you just take 

00:00:14.969 --> 00:00:18.599
Tony's as a case study for a moment,

00:00:21.029 --> 00:00:22.800
what is it that faith

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.390
does in these circumstances? We analyze it as opposed to just accept

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:29.960
what he's done.

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:34.220
And, actually Saint Paul is a very good example of this. In fact, to use 

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:35.440
from a different religion  

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:41.150
the Prophet Mohammed is very good example of this

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:42.680
the first thing is

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:47.130
you've got to

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:49.480
be prepared

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:51.100
to believe that something

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:54.130
that's wrong can be changed into something that's right.

00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:57.460
You know, a lot of people look at the world, they don't dispute there's terrible things but they

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:00.870
just say, you know, 

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:03.160
but actually what faith

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:03.770
does is it

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:05.580
gives you a

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:06.900
in a sense that

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:08.140
things can be

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:09.340
changed.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:10.710
They don't have to be like that.

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:15.010
They can be changed. There's a sense of possibility that comes with it.

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:18.880
The second thing I think that comes with it is a willingness to think differently

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:19.680
and anew

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:22.260
and to challenge conventional wisdom.

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:28.490
I mean, I think what's interesting about all

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:29.650
the, all

00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:30.829
the great

00:01:30.829 --> 00:01:31.720
prophets -  

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:35.550
and that's taken in the broadest sense certainly with the Abrahamic faiths -

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:38.260
but all the great prophets have one thing in common,

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:41.670
which is absolutely fascinating in the sense that now there's a, there's a kind of doctrine 

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:42.270
about them

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:45.780
and that is they were all challenging conventional thinking.

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:49.320
You know, Saint Paul absolutely to radically, obviously, but not just  

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:51.550
in relation to

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:55.420
Judaism and Christianity, but actually in relation to the practices of

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:58.460
Judaism, some of which were kept, but some of which were altered.  

00:01:59.649 --> 00:02:02.130
Saint Peter and Cornelius likewise. So there's that as well,

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:05.320
but again you could go

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:07.480
across the

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:09.970
religious movements and you look at any of the great teachers,

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:13.540
you look at any of the great teachers

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:15.809
with, for example, Hindu religion, 

00:02:15.809 --> 00:02:17.700
they're all people who are challenging

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:19.539
the conventional wisdom.

00:02:19.539 --> 00:02:23.549
And I think the third thing is that it gives you is a determination to keep going

00:02:23.549 --> 00:02:27.929
in the face of adversity and, and

00:02:27.929 --> 00:02:30.409
to recognize that

00:02:30.409 --> 00:02:35.329
there is something more important than the transient good opinion

00:02:35.329 --> 00:02:37.799
of any particular time.

00:02:37.799 --> 00:02:39.239
And that is what

00:02:39.239 --> 00:02:41.639
I think.

00:02:41.639 --> 00:02:43.389
Faith, faith

00:02:43.389 --> 00:02:46.749
can only mean something if at certain

00:02:46.749 --> 00:02:47.689
vital moment,

00:02:47.689 --> 00:02:50.949
for all your weaknesses

00:02:50.949 --> 00:02:53.779
and

00:02:53.779 --> 00:02:54.789
limitations,

00:02:54.789 --> 00:02:57.489
you are prepared to surrender self.

00:02:57.489 --> 00:02:59.209
That's why, that's what

00:02:59.209 --> 00:03:01.760
Islam, Islam means; it means surrender to God.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:04.359
Right, so, it's, it's a, it's a, that, that is

00:03:04.359 --> 00:03:08.919
that is a concept that I think is dramatically important in faith

00:03:08.919 --> 00:03:10.019
and so what leads

00:03:10.019 --> 00:03:16.409
him to do something that would be very: A)

00:03:16.409 --> 00:03:18.569
that is thinking that he can actually change something,

00:03:18.569 --> 00:03:20.319
when most people have said "Well, how

00:03:20.319 --> 00:03:24.479
you going to get them to change that?" B) he does it  

00:03:24.479 --> 00:03:28.129
in certain circumstances where he's prepared to do something radically different and challenge conventional

00:03:28.129 --> 00:03:29.449
wisdom.

00:03:29.449 --> 00:03:30.859
So he keeps at it.

00:03:30.859 --> 00:03:35.180
Now I think those are, those are really important aspects in the, in the way that faith can

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:36.189
interact with

00:03:36.189 --> 00:03:38.799
the way that you, you, you do things and that's

00:03:38.799 --> 00:03:41.279
and that's what you notice.

00:03:41.279 --> 00:03:45.499
And, it's what you notice. I mean I'm a, I am a, a,  

00:03:45.499 --> 00:03:50.009
you know many ways, as a, as a practicing politician,

00:03:50.009 --> 00:03:51.839
you know, when you're in government,

00:03:51.839 --> 00:03:55.509
you're taking decisions the whole time, right.

00:03:55.509 --> 00:04:00.089
And let's be honest about it, some of these decisions you're taking on a very

00:04:00.089 --> 00:04:03.379
practical transactional basis, right.

00:04:03.379 --> 00:04:05.619
And we go this way rather than that way. Let's suss it out. 

00:04:05.619 --> 00:04:06.829
We got

00:04:06.829 --> 00:04:10.260
these problems that, you know, that is the business of politics and it's there,

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:14.049
but there are always crucial moments

00:04:14.049 --> 00:04:15.809
in which,

00:04:15.809 --> 00:04:18.169
actually it's not transactional issue,

00:04:18.169 --> 00:04:22.360
it can be either a transformational issue or it's certainly an issue of great principle and it's

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:23.799
at that point

00:04:23.799 --> 00:04:26.539
that you, you work out within yourself

00:04:26.539 --> 00:04:28.779
whether you're prepared

00:04:28.779 --> 00:04:33.649
to surrender what might be actually a short-term personal self-interest to something bigger.

00:04:33.649 --> 00:04:37.309
So, when Tony decides, you know, they may describe as a

00:04:37.309 --> 00:04:39.150
nut case and finish him off

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:44.099
but he nonetheless decides to do it. Now

00:04:44.099 --> 00:04:49.340
the really difficult thing is if they had finished you off. Where would you be now? But actually

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:51.529
the fact is you would still be doing the same thing.

00:04:51.529 --> 00:04:53.669
Now that's, that's what it gives you and that's the difference, I think.

